  0%|          | 0/204 [00:00<?, ?it/s]messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nA person is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor. All people who are Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players. All Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities. All professional tennis players are athletes. If a person is a celebrity then they are well paid. If a person is an athlete then they are famous. All well-paid people live in tax havens. If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in well paid.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Djokovic lives in a tax haven.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Djokovic lives in a tax haven" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context provides several premises:\n\n1.A person is either a Grand Slam champion or an Oscar-nominated actor.\n2.All Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players.\n3.All Oscar-nominated actors are celebrities.\n4.All professional tennis players are athletes.\n5.If a person is a celebrity, then they are well-paid.\n6.If a person is an athlete, then they are famous.\n7.All well-paid people live in tax havens.\n8.If Djokovic is famous and is an athlete, then Djokovic lives in a well-paid place.\n\nThe question is about whether Djokovic lives in a tax haven.\n\nLet me start by categorizing Djokovic.From premise 2, Grand Slam champions are professional tennis players.Djokovic is a well-known tennis player and has won multiple Grand Slams, so he\'s a Grand Slam champion.Therefore, he\'s not a professional tennis player (premise 2). From premise 4, not all professional tennis players are athletes, so Djokovic is not an athlete.\n\nNow, from premise 6, if someone is not an athlete, they are famous. So Djokovic is not famous.\n\nNext, premise 8 says if Djokovic is not famous and not an athlete, then he lives in a well-paid place. Since we\'ve established he\'s neither famous nor an athlete, this premise directly applies to him. Therefore, he does not live in a well-paid place.\n\nFrom premise 7, not all well-paid people live in tax havens. So if Djokovic is not well-paid, he must not live in a tax haven.\n\nPutting it all together: Djokovic is not a professional tennis player, thus not an athlete, making him not famous. Since he\'s not famous and not an athlete, he\'s not well-paid, and not all well-paid people live in tax havens. Therefore, the statement that Djokovic lives in a tax haven is false.</think>.\n\nThe statement is **True** because Djokovic, being a professional tennis player and thus an athlete, is famous. As an athlete, he is well-paid, and all well-paid individuals live in tax havens. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
