nBut earlier, I thought of a case where C is true and B is false, which would make \u00acB \u2192 \u00acC false, but that would mean that C \u2192 B is false, which contradicts the contrapositive.\n\nWait, no, because if C is true and B is false, then C \u2192 B is false, which would mean that the contrapositive \u00acB \u2192 \u00acC is also false, but that's impossible because the contrapositive must be true if C \u2192 B is true.\n\nTherefore, such a case cannot exist because premise 3 ensures that if C is true and W is true, then B is true, but if W is false, C can be true without B being true.\n\nWait, no, premise 3 only says that if he can compose and wants to, he will compose, but it doesn't say anything about if he can compose but doesn't want to.\n\nSo, in that case, he can compose (C) but doesn't want to (\u00acW), so he won't compose (\u00acB).So, in this case, C is true, B is false, so \u00acB is true, and \u00acC is false, making the implication \u00acB \u2192 \u00acC false.\n\nBut according to the contrapositive, if C \u2192 B is true, then \u00acB \u2192 \u00acC must be true, which is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the only way for the contrapositive to hold is if there are no cases where C is true and B is false, which is not the case because premise 3 allows for that.\n\nTherefore, the statement is not necessarily true, and the correct answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but I'm getting conflicting conclusions here.\n\nLet me try to think of it in terms of logical implications.\n\nPremise 3: (C \u2227 W) \u2192 B.\n\nThis can be rewritten as: If C and W are both true, then B is true.\n\nBut if C is true and W is false, then B can be either true or false.\n\nTherefore, in the case where C is true and W is false, B can be false.\n\nSo, in that case, \u00acB is true, and \u00acC is false, making the implication \u00acB \u2192 \u00acC false.\n\nTherefore, the statement is not always true, so it's false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) False.\n</think>\n