messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me start by carefully reading the context and understanding each part.\n\nThe context is a series of conditional statements connected by "if...then" clauses.Let me break them down one by one.\n\n1.If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.\n2.If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.\n3.People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.\n4.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.\n5.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n6.If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\nNow, the question is about whether Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.The options are True, False, or Uncertain.\n\nLet me try to connect the statements.The first statement says that if someone works in a student job, they need to earn money for tuition.The second connects ordering takeout to working in student jobs.The third says people either order takeout or enjoy dining hall meals.The fourth and fifth talk about dining hall enjoyers not being picky and spending time socializing.The sixth is a specific statement about Mary.\n\nWait, the sixth statement is a conditional: If Mary works in student jobs and needs to earn money for tuition, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.So, if both conditions are true (she works in student jobs and needs money), then she is neither picky nor needs the money.That seems a bit confusing.Let me parse it again.\n\nThe structure is: If A and B, then C. So, if Mary works in student jobs (A) and needs to earn money (B), then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money (C).So, if both A and B are true, then C is true.But wait, that would mean that if she works in student jobs and needs money, she doesn\'t need the money anymore.That seems contradictory.Maybe I\'m misinterpreting it.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the statement is saying that if Mary works in student jobs and needs money, then she is neither picky nor needs the money.So, the conclusion is that she doesn\'t need the money.So, if both A and B are true, then she doesn\'t need the money.That would mean that the statement is saying that if she works in student jobs and needs money, then she doesn\'t need the money.That seems contradictory, so perhaps the only way this makes sense is if the premise is false.\n\nWait, maybe the statement is saying that if Mary works in student jobs and needs money, then she is neither picky nor needs the money.So, the conclusion is that she doesn\'t need the money.So, the statement is asserting that if she works in student jobs and needs money, then she doesn\'t need the money.The statement is true because the conditional in the context implies that if she works in student jobs and needs money, she doesn\'t need it.Therefore, the answer should be A) True. </think>.\n\nThe statement "Mary needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition" is **False** based on the given context. Therefore, my final option is: ']]  9%|9         | 19/204 [02:31<1:19:25, 25.76s/it] 10%|9         | 20/204 [02:33<57:54, 18.89s/it]  
