ives, then it\'s rated >9 and doesn\'t provide take-out.\n\nBut from the premises, we\'ve established that if Subway has N, it can\'t have R>9 because P would require R>9, but having N would mean it\'s not R>9, which contradicts P → R>9.So, the situation where Subway has N is impossible because it would require both P and ¬R>9, which is a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, the antecedent (T ∧ N) can never be true for Subway because having N would lead to a contradiction.Therefore, the implication is vacuously true because the antecedent is always false.\n\nWait, but let me think again.If the antecedent is always false, then the implication is always true.So, the statement "If P, then Q" is true if P is always false.\n\nIn this case, if Subway cannot have both T and N, then the statement is true because the implication holds when P is false.\n\nBut hurry, isn\'t that not the case?Let me not check.\n\nFrom the conclusions, we don\'t have that unless a restaurant lacks N, then it\'s R>9.But not from premise 5, Subway is neither R>9 nor P. So, if it is R>9, it can\'t be P. From premise 4, P → R>9.So, if it\'s not P, then not R>9.But if it\'s not P, then not R>9, which would indicate it\'s not recommended (from premise 2), so it might have N (from premise 1).Hence, if Subway doesn\'t have N, it might be P, hence it might have R>9, but from premise 5, it might not be either R>9 or P. So, if it is R>9, it could be P, which does not necessitate R>9, which supports the fact that it has N. Hence, the only way for Subway to lack N is if it\'s R>9 and P, but from premise 5, it must not be either R>9 or P. Therefore, it\'s possible for Subway to have N.\n\nTherefore, the consequent (T ∧ N) is possible for Subway.So, the statement "If (T ∧ N), then (R>9 ∧ ¬T)" is not vacuously true because the consequent can sometimes be true.\n\nHurry, and the statement is: If (T ∧ N), then (R>9 ∧ ¬T).So, if the consequent is possible, the implication is false.\n\nBut hurry, the statement is about the