messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nBadults is a British Sitcom series, starring members of Pappy\'s. Badults piloted in July 2013 on BBC Three. The Working title "The Secret Dude Society" was used for Badults. Andrew Collins was the script editor for Badults\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? No members of Pappy\'s have starred for a show piloting on BBC Two or BBC Three.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me start by reading the context carefully.\n\nThe context says that Badults is a British Sitcom series starring members of Pappy\'s.It was piloted in July 2013 on BBC Three.The working title was "The Secret Dude Society," and Andrew Collins was the script editor.\n\nNow, the question is asking if the statement "No members of Pappy\'s have starred for a show piloting on BBC Two or BBC Three" is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nFirst, I know that Badults is on BBC Three, and it\'s a sitcom.The show\'s members are from Pappy\'s, so that means at least some members of Pappy\'s have starred in a show on BBC Three.So, the statement says "no members" have starred on BBC Two or BBC Three.But since Badults is on BBC Three and features Pappy\'s members, that directly contradicts the statement.Therefore, the statement is false because there is at least one show (Badults) where Pappy\'s members starred on BBC Three.\nThe statement claims that no Pappy\'s members have appeared on BBC Two or BBC Three, but we can confirm that some have starred on BBC Two, making the statement true.</think>.\n\nThe statement claims that no members of Pappy\'s have starred in a show piloted on BBC Two or BBC Three. However, Badults, a show featuring Pappy\'s members, was piloted on BBC Three. Therefore, the statement is false. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
