messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement about Mary is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me start by carefully reading the context and the question again.\n\nThe context is a series of conditional statements.Let me list them out to make it clearer:\n\n1.If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.\n2.If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.\n3.People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.\n4.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.\n5.If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n6.If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\nThe question is asking whether the statement "Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls."is true, false, or uncertain.\n\nFirst, I\'ll try to break down the statement.It\'s a logical OR statement: either Mary is not a picky eater, or if she is a picky eater, then she spends a lot of time eating and socializing in the dining halls.\n\nTo analyze this, I need to see what we know about Mary from the context.Let\'s look at the context again.\n\nFrom statement 6: If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money for tuition, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.Wait, that seems a bit confusing.Let me parse it again.It says, "If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition."\n\nHmm, that seems a bit contradictory.If she works in student jobs and needs to earn money, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money.So, if she works and needs the money, she isn\'t picky, and she doesn\'t need the money.That seems contradictory because if she needs the money, she should be earning it.Maybe I\'m misinterpreting it.\n\nWait, perhaps it\'s better to represent this as logical implications.Let me try to represent each statement as implications.\n\nStatement 1: If A (works in student jobs), then B (needs money).\nStatement 2: If C (orders takeout), then A (works in student jobs).\nStatement 3: A or C (either works in student jobs or orders takeout).\nStatement 4: If D (enjoys dining hall meals), then not E (not picky eater).\nStatement 5: If D, then F (spends a lot of time eating and socializing).\nStatement 6: If (A and B), then not E and not B.Wait, that can\'t be right.Let me parse statement 6 again.\n\nStatement 6: If Mary works in student jobs on campus (A) and needs to earn money (B), then Mary is neither picky (not E) nor needs to earn money (not B).So, if A and B, then not E and not B.That seems odd because if she needs to earn money (B), then she shouldn\'t need to earn money (not B).That\'s a contradiction unless B is false.\n\nWait, perhaps I\'m misapplying it.Let me think again.The statement is: If Mary works in student jobs on campus (A) and needs to earn money (B), then she is neither picky (not E) nor needs to earn money (not B).So, if A and B, then not E and not B.But if B is true, then not B is false, making the entire conclusion false.So, the implication would be that if A and B, then a contradiction, which is impossible.Therefore, the only way for the implication to hold is if A and B are false.So, if Mary does not work in student jobs or she doesn\'t need money, then the statement holds.\n\nBut I\'m not sure if that\'s the right way to interpret it.Maybe it\'s better to think that if Mary works in student jobs and needs money, then she is not picky and doesn\'t need money.But that seems contradictory because if she needs money, she should be earning it.So perhaps the only way this makes sense is if Mary doesn\'t need money, so she doesn\'t have to work in student jobs.But I\'m getting confused here.\n\nLet me try to approach it differently.Let\'s see what we can infer about Mary.\n\nFrom statement 6: If Mary works in student jobs (A) and needs money (B), then she is neither picky (not E) nor needs money (not B).So, if A and B, then not E and not B.But if B is true, then not B is false, making the conclusion impossible.Therefore, the only way for the implication to hold is if A is false or B is false.So, either Mary doesn\'t work in student jobs, or she doesn\'t need money, or both.\n\nBut wait, the question is about Mary\'s status regarding pickiness and her dining habits.Let\'s see.\n\nFrom statement 3: People either order takeout (C) or enjoy dining hall meals (D).So, C or D.\n\nFrom statement 4: If D, then not E (not picky).\n\nFrom statement 5: If D, then F (spends time eating and socializing).\n\nSo, if Mary enjoys dining hall meals (D), then she is not picky (not E) and spends time eating and socializing (F).\n\nNow, the statement we\'re evaluating is: Mary either is not a picky eater (not E) or, if she is, then she spends a lot of time eating and catching up with friends in the dining halls (F).\n\nIn logical terms, this is (not E) OR (E → F).\n\nWe need to determine if this statement is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.\n\nLet me consider the possibilities.\n\nMary is a picky eater. She enjoys dining hall meals and spends time eating and socializing. If Mary enjoys dining hall meals, she is picky and does not order takeout. If people order takeout, they do not work in student jobs. Mary does not need money, so she doesn\'t work in student jobs. Therefore, Mary is not a picky eater, does not order takeout, and does not work in student jobs. The statement is false because Mary is not picky. The answer should be B) False.</think>.\n\nThe statement is true because Mary is not a picky eater, as deduced from the context. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 56%|#####6    | 115/204 [13:03<03:04,  2.07s/it]
