messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today. Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers. Employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home. If an employee has lunch at home, then he/she is working remotely from home. All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home. No managers work remotely from home. James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James has lunch in the company.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "James has lunch in the company" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that all employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today.So, if someone schedules such a meeting, they must be present at the office.Now, it also mentions that everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers.That means if an employee has lunch at the company, they must be scheduling meetings, and thus they must appear today.\n\nNext, employees either have lunch in the company or at home.If they have lunch at home, they\'re working remotely.The context also states that all employees in other countries work remotely from home.Additionally, no managers work remotely.So, managers must either be in the company or not working at all today.\n\nNow, the key part about James: he\'s either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears today.So, if James is a manager, he\'s at the office.If he\'s not a manager, he might be either at the office or not, but the context doesn\'t explicitly say.\n\nThe question is whether James has lunch in the company.Let\'s consider the possibilities.\n\nIf James is a manager, he must be at the office today because managers don\'t work remotely.Since he\'s a manager, he would have lunch at the company.So, in this case, the statement is true.\n\nIf James is not a manager, he could either be at the office or working from home.If he\'s at the office, he has lunch there, making the statement true.If he\'s working from home, he has lunch at home, making the statement false.\n\nWait, but the context says that all employees who are in other countries work remotely.So, if James is not a manager, he might be in another country, thus working from home.But the context doesn\'t specify where James is.He could be in the same country as the company, in which case he might be at the office or home.But since the context doesn\'t limit his location, we don\'t know for sure.\n\nHowever, the initial statement says that all employees who schedule meetings appear in the company.Regardless of James\'s managerial status, it is clear that he always schedules meetings and works from the office, even if he has lunch in the company. The information provided is sufficient to determine his role and whereabouts, making the answer certain.</think>.\n\nThe statement about James having lunch in the company is uncertain because we don\'t know if he\'s a manager (in which case he must be there) or not, and if not, whether he\'s working from home or the office. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
