messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nAll people who have a wedding are people who have at least one child. All people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon are people who have a wedding. All weddings that occur in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon. Some weddings in July are well-attended. If John has at least one child, then John does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding. If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then John has a wedding in July or, if not, then John travels to Paris for their honeymoon.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? John does not have a wedding that is well-attended.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "John does not have a wedding that is well-attended" is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break down the information step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that all people who have a wedding are people who have at least one child.So, if someone has a wedding, they must have at least one child.That means John, if he has a wedding, must have at least one child.But wait, the next part says that if John has at least one child, then he does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding.Hmm, that\'s a bit confusing.So, if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding and doesn\'t go to Paris.But if he doesn\'t have a child, maybe he could have a wedding and go to Paris?\n\nWait, let me parse that again.The statement is: If John has at least one child, then he does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding.So, having a child implies no wedding and no Paris trip.But if he doesn\'t have a child, what happens?The context doesn\'t explicitly say, but it\'s possible that without a child, he could have a wedding and go to Paris.\n\nNext, the context mentions that all people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon are people who have a wedding.So, traveling to Paris implies they had a wedding.Also, all weddings that occur in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon.So, if a wedding is in July, the person must have gone to Paris for their honeymoon, which in turn means they had a wedding and have at least one child?Wait, no, because earlier it said that if someone has a wedding, they have at least one child.But if John has a wedding in July, he must have gone to Paris, which implies he has a wedding and thus has a child.But wait, the first part says if John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding.So that seems contradictory.\n\nWait, maybe I\'m getting this wrong.Let me try to structure the information.\n\n1.All people with a wedding have at least one child.\n2.All people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon have a wedding.\n3.All weddings in July belong to people who travel to Paris for their honeymoon.\n4.Some weddings in July are well-attended.\n5.If John has at least one child, then he does not travel to Paris for his honeymoon and does not have a wedding.\n6.If John has a wedding that is well-attended, then either he has a wedding in July, or if not, he travels to Paris for his honeymoon.\n\nSo, let\'s analyze point 5: If John has a child, he doesn\'t have a wedding and doesn\'t go to Paris.So, John having a child means he doesn\'t have a wedding.Therefore, if John has a wedding, he must not have any children.Because if he had a child, he couldn\'t have a wedding.\n\nNow, point 6: If John has a well-attended wedding, then either he has a wedding in July, or if not, he goes to Paris.So, a well-attended wedding implies either a July wedding or a Paris trip.\n\nBut wait, if John has a well-attended wedding, and if he has a wedding, he must not have any children (from point 5).So, he doesn\'t have children, so he can have a wedding.Now, if his wedding is well-attended, then either he got married in July or he went to Paris.\n\nBut all weddings in July are for people who go to Paris.So, if his wedding is in July, he must have gone to Paris.Alternatively, if his wedding isn\'t in July, then he would have gone to Paris anyway because all Paris travelers have a wedding, and all July weddings go to Paris.\n\nWait, but if his wedding is in July, he goes to Paris.If his wedding is not in July, he still goes to Paris because all Paris travelers have a wedding, and if he didn\'t have a wedding, he wouldn\'t be going.So, regardless of whether his wedding is in July or not, he would be going to Paris.\n\nBut the statement in point 6 is that if he has a well-attended wedding, then he either has a July wedding or he goes to Paris.So, either way, he\'s going to Paris.But if he has a well-attended wedding, he must have a wedding, which means he doesn\'t have any children (from point 5).So, he can have a wedding, and if it\'s well-attended, he goes to Paris.\n\nNow, the question is whether John does not have a well-attended wedding.So, is it true, false, or uncertain?\n\nLet me consider possibilities.\nJohn always has a well-attended wedding, whether he has a child or not. The statement "John does not have a wedding that is well-attended" is always false.</think>.\n\nThe statement "John does not have a wedding that is well-attended" cannot be definitively determined as true or false based on the given context. It depends on whether John has a child or not, which is not specified. Therefore, the answer is uncertain. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 72%|#######1  | 146/204 [19:21<08:20,  8.62s/it] 72%|#######2  | 147/204 [19:21<06:03,  6.38s/it]
