messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n####\n# Context:\nOn some Mondays at 8 pm, roses are given out on TV. Every rose given out on TV is on the Bachelor. Everything on the Bachelor portrays the lives of real people. All shows portraying the lives of real people are reality TV shows. Breaking Bad is not a reality TV show.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? If roses are given out during Breaking Bad, then it is on Monday at 8 pm.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', '<think>\nOkay, so I need to figure out whether the statement "If roses are given out during Breaking Bad, then it is on Monday at 8 pm."is true, false, or uncertain based on the given context.Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, let\'s look at the context provided.The context has a few key points:\n\n1.On some Mondays at 8 pm, roses are given out on TV.\n2.Every rose given out on TV during these Mondays is on the Bachelor.\n3.The Bachelor is a show that portrays the lives of real people.\n4.All shows that portray the lives of real people are reality TV shows.\n5.Breaking Bad is not a reality TV show.\n\nSo, the context is telling us about the relationship between roses, the Bachelor, reality TV shows, and Breaking Bad.Now, the question is about whether roses given out during Breaking Bad would imply it\'s on Monday at 8 pm.\n\nLet me parse the statement: "If roses are given out during Breaking Bad, then it is on Monday at 8 pm."So, this is a conditional statement.In logic terms, it\'s of the form If P, then Q.Here, P is "roses are given out during Breaking Bad," and Q is "it is on Monday at 8 pm."\n\nFrom the context, we know that roses are given out on the Bachelor, which is a reality TV show.Breaking Bad, however, is not a reality TV show.So, if roses are given out during Breaking Bad, that would mean that Breaking Bad is a reality TV show, which contradicts the given information.\n\nWait, but the context says that every rose given out on TV during Mondays at 8 pm is on the Bachelor, which is a reality TV show.So, the only time roses are given out on TV is during the Bachelor, which is a reality TV show.Breaking Bad is not a reality TV show, so during Breaking Bad, roses are not given out on TV.\n\nTherefore, the statement "If roses are given out during Breaking Bad, then it is on Monday at 8 pm" is based on a false premise because roses aren\'t given out during Breaking Bad.So, the implication is of the form If False, then Q.In logic, if the premise is false, the entire statement is considered true because an implication is only false when the premise is true and the conclusion is false.\n\nWait, but that\'s a bit confusing.Let me think again.The statement is false because the premise is impossible to meet.</think>.\n\nThe statement is true because the premise (roses being given out during Breaking Bad) is impossible, making the implication vacuously true. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
